---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="EBITDA Defense Playbook — North End Technology"
  description="Protect margin through a platform transition without gutting the product roadmap."
>
  <section class="playbook-hero section-alt">
    <div class="wrapper">
      <p class="eyebrow">Playbook</p>
      <h1>EBITDA Defense</h1>
      <p>
        A sponsor-facing framework for protecting margin during a platform
        consolidation, without freezing the roadmap or gutting engineering capacity.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrapper playbook-body">
      <h2>Why margin erodes during convergence</h2>
      <p>
        Every platform convergence effort carries a hidden cost curve: duplicate
        infrastructure runs in parallel with the new shared layer, product teams
        maintain two integration surfaces, and support load spikes as customers
        migrate. Left unmanaged, this shows up directly in EBITDA before it shows up
        anywhere else.
      </p>

      <h2>The defense sequence</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Freeze the blast radius.</strong> Identify every cost center touched by the convergence before work begins, not after the first budget review.</li>
        <li><strong>Sunset on a fixed clock.</strong> Attach an explicit decommission date to every legacy system kept alive during migration — open-ended parallel-run is the single largest margin leak.</li>
        <li><strong>Instrument cost per shared service.</strong> Make infrastructure spend attributable to the platform layer, not buried inside individual product P&amp;Ls.</li>
        <li><strong>Report the defense, not just the build.</strong> Sponsors underwrite discipline. A monthly view of cost avoided is as valuable as a view of features shipped.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What sponsors actually check</h2>
      <p>
        Diligence teams look for one thing above all: whether cost decisions during a
        technical transition were made deliberately or reactively. A documented
        defense sequence, run consistently across every planning cycle, is the
        evidence they're looking for.
      </p>

      <div class="playbook-cta">
        <a href={`${base}/#contact`} class="btn btn-primary">Discuss this playbook</a>
        <a href={`${base}/#playbooks`} class="back-link">&larr; Back to all playbooks</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
